<!--
  /tooling — the in-editor + agent surface for SUI. Three pieces wire
  together: the LSP server, the in-browser LSP companion, and the
  compiler. Agents and IDEs land on the LSP; humans land on the
  in-editor wiring.
-->
<div class="doc tooling-doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guides</p>
  <h1>Tooling</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SUI ships a full LSP, a browser-side analyzer, and a compiler CLI.
    Three surfaces, one grammar — agents and IDEs read
    <code>.sui</code> the same way the SUI compiler does, so the
    diagnostic a human sees in their editor matches the diagnostic an
    agent sees in its tool loop.
  </p>

  <h2>1. The compiler</h2>
  <p>
    <code>sui-compiler build &lt;file.sui&gt;</code> is the source of truth. It
    runs the same Rust grammar the in-process editor and the browser
    analyzer use. Use it from CI, from a pre-commit hook, or from an
    agent's tool loop. A non-zero exit is a build failure.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.cli}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>2. The LSP (IDE)</h2>
  <p>
    Point your editor at the <code>sui-lsp</code> binary via a project
    <code>.lsp.json</code>. The server speaks the Language Server Protocol
    over stdio, surfaces diagnostics with 1-based line/column positions,
    and ships completions keyed off the active scope.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Project config — <code>.lsp.json</code> at the workspace root:</strong>
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.lspJson}></sui-codeblock>
  <p>
    Most editors pick this up automatically. For VS Code: drop a
    <code>sui-lsp</code> extension reference. For Neovim:
    <code>vim.lsp.start(&#123; cmd = "sui-lsp" &#125;)</code>. For Helix: the
    default config matches the protocol out of the box. For agents: the
    same binary is the LSP endpoint — no separate "agent mode" needed.
  </p>

  <h2>3. The browser-side analyzer</h2>
  <p>
    For docs, the playground, and any in-app code editor, the
    <code>@ship/sui-lsp-browser</code> package runs the same Rust
    grammar in a wasm bundle. The <code>&lt;sui-code-editor&gt;</code>
    element on the tutorial page wires it through
    <code>host.setDiagnostics</code> + <code>host.setCompletions</code>
    on every debounced doc change.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.browser}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>How they relate</h2>
  <p>
    One grammar. Three transports. The CLI runs the compiler synchronously
    and prints diagnostics. The LSP runs the same compiler on
    every keystroke and ships the results over stdio. The browser
    analyzer runs the same compiler in a wasm module and pushes
    diagnostics into the editor's lint gutter. An agent reading a
    <code>.sui</code> file via the CLI sees the same errors a human
    sees in the IDE; the wire formats differ, the parser does not.
  </p>

  <h2>Agent loop</h2>
  <p>
    Agents should treat <code>sui compile &lt;file.sui&gt;</code> as the
    authoritative lint pass. The same diagnostic shape the LSP returns
    is what <code>compile</code> writes to stderr — a stable contract
    to grep on. Run it before declaring a SUI change done.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.agent}></sui-codeblock>

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/composition">← Composition</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/compare">Compare →</a>
  </div>
</div>
